      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nSharing, measuring and comparing have been part of human life since the beginning of humanity: dividing food among the members of a family, distributing seeds in a field, or organizing the days of a calendar.\n\nWhenever we share something in equal parts, we are dividing. Division also keeps a close relationship with another operation that we already know well: multiplication.\n\nBefore continuing, let us reflect:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Two connected operations</b><br /><br />If we know that 4 × 5 = 20, what can we say about 20 ÷ 4 and 20 ÷ 5? Are multiplication and division related? How?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_multiplication_division",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplication and division",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent4\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Each row of the table presents a multiplication and, at the same time, two divisions. For example, the row 4 × 5 = 20 tells us that:\n- 20 ÷ 4 = 5, because 4 × 5 = 20;\n- 20 ÷ 5 = 4, because 5 × 4 = 20.\n\nThis happens because division is the inverse operation of multiplication (<htcite0>): if a × b = c and neither a nor b is zero, then c ÷ a = b and c ÷ b = a.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ae53cf73-8e7d-4a2c-80a3-1cb79b7c48bc",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Inversion",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>a</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">x</mo><mn>b</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>c</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">⇔</mo><mn>c</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">÷</mo><mn>a</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>b</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: The relationship between multiplication and division.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In words: if a × b = c, then c ÷ a = b and c ÷ b = a.\n\nThe multiplication table is therefore a powerful tool for dividing: when we want to divide a number by 4, we look in the table of 4 for the multiplication that produces that number (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ad9516ac-2893-4649-8d62-7f9cfe69cc64",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Understanding the Multiplication Table (4 and 5)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_long_division",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The long division",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "When the numbers are larger, we can use the **long division algorithm**. It consists of dividing digit by digit, starting from the highest place value, and obtaining the quotient one digit at a time (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4742a2d6-a3c5-4883-beb5-8ed0240fa9a7",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Long Division",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "The parts of a division receive special names:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div class=\"htLongDivisionAlgorithms\"><div class=\"htLongDivisionAlgorithm\"><table class=\"htLongDivisionParts\" style=\"margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; font-weight: none; border-collapse: collapse;\"><tr><td>Dividend </td><td style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid black; border-left: 2px solid black;\"> Divisor </td></tr> <tr><td>Remainder </td><td> Quotient</td></tr></table><p class=\"desc\"><b>Algorithm 1</b>: Parts of the long division with the result below.</p></div><div class=\"htLongDivisionAlgorithm\"><table class=\"htLongDivisionTop htLongDivisionPartsTable\" role=\"presentation\"><tr><td></td><td class=\"htLongDivisionQuotient\">Quotient</td></tr><tr><td class=\"htLongDivisionDivisor\">Divisor</td><td class=\"htLongDivisionDividend\">Dividend</td></tr><tr><td></td><td class=\"htLongDivisionStep\">Remainder</td></tr></table><p class=\"desc\"><b>Algorithm 2</b>: Parts of the long division with the result on top.</p></div></div>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "The same division can also be written in a single line:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>Dividend</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>Quotient</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">x</mo><mn>Divisor</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>Remainder</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 2</b>: The division written as a formula.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "A long division can be written with the result below or with the result on top. Choose the one you prefer for the following calculation:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"htLongDivisionFormat\"><b>Division format:</b> <button type=\"button\" class=\"htLongDivisionFormatBtn active\" data-htldformat=\"below\">Result below</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"htLongDivisionFormatBtn\" data-htldformat=\"top\">Result on top</button></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "As an example, let us divide 29 by 4. The number 29 has 2 tens and 9 units. We start with the highest place value, the tens: since 2 is less than 4, there is no digit for the tens place in the quotient. We then take the 2 tens together with the 9 units, which gives 29 units. Dividing 29 into groups of 4, we get 7 groups (7 × 4 = 28) and 1 unit left over. The quotient is 7, with remainder 1:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"htLongDivision\" style=\"width: 30%; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; font-weight: none; border-collapse: collapse;\"><tr><td>&nbsp; 29 </td><td style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid black; border-left: 2px solid black;\"> 4 </td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid black;\">- 28 </td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 1 </td><td> &nbsp; </td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: Division of 29 by 4.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In the representation with **result below**, the quotient appears below the divisor, as in **Calculation 1**. In the representation with **result on top**, the quotient appears above the dividend. To divide, we follow the same steps in both representations:\n- we divide the highest place value by the divisor;\n- we multiply the quotient digit by the divisor and subtract the result;\n- we bring down the next place value and repeat until we reach the units.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Real proof\n\nTo confirm the result, we use the relationship between multiplication and division from **Formula 2**: division is the inverse of multiplication (<htcite0>). If the quotient is 7 and the divisor is 4, then 7 × 4 = 28. Adding the remainder, 1, we obtain 28 + 1 = 29, which is exactly the dividend. Since we recovered the dividend, the quotient 7 and the remainder 1 are correct.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ae53cf73-8e7d-4a2c-80a3-1cb79b7c48bc",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Inversion",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>7</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">x</mo><mn>4</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>29</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 2</b>: Real proof of the division of 29 by 4.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_squares",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplication and division with squares",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Let us see how multiplication and division work with squares (<htcite0>). Consider a rectangle formed by 3 rows of 4 squares:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a648cecf-238e-4294-a012-3830ff833d67",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Drawing the multiplication",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 248 184\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:248px; display:block; margin:0 auto;\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Rectangle with 3 rows of 4 squares, 12 squares in total\"><title>Three rows of four squares</title><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"64\" y=\"0\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"128\" y=\"0\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"192\" y=\"0\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"64\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"64\" y=\"64\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"128\" y=\"64\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"192\" y=\"64\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"128\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"64\" y=\"128\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"128\" y=\"128\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"192\" y=\"128\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></svg><b>Figure 1</b>: A rectangle with 3 rows of 4 squares.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Counting the squares, we find 12. Therefore, 3 × 4 = 12: that is multiplication (<htcite0>).\n\nThe same 12 squares can be shared in different ways:\n- in 3 equal rows, each row has 12 ÷ 3 = 4 squares;\n- in 4 equal columns, each column has 12 ÷ 4 = 3 squares.\n\nMultiplication and division describe the same rectangle from different points of view.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d862242c-0538-4b18-8b32-4a84d4a5858e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now, suppose we want to share the same 12 squares in groups of 5:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 328 240\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:328px; display:block; margin:0 auto;\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Twelve squares grouped in fives: two groups of five and two squares left over\"><title>Two groups of five squares and two squares left over</title><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"328\" height=\"72\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"8 4\"/><rect x=\"8\" y=\"8\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"72\" y=\"8\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"136\" y=\"8\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"200\" y=\"8\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"264\" y=\"8\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"84\" width=\"328\" height=\"72\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"8 4\"/><rect x=\"8\" y=\"92\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"72\" y=\"92\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"136\" y=\"92\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"200\" y=\"92\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"264\" y=\"92\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"8\" y=\"172\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"72\" y=\"172\" width=\"56\" height=\"56\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></svg><b>Figure 2</b>: Twelve squares grouped in fives: two complete groups of five and two squares left over.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "We can form 2 complete groups and 2 squares remain: 12 = 2 × 5 + 2, that is, 12 ÷ 5 = 2 with remainder 2. The remainder is the part that is not enough to form another group.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text, we learned that multiplication and division are inverse operations: each multiplication fact gives us two divisions, and the multiplication table helps us divide.\n\nWe also met the long division algorithm, learned the names of its parts, and saw that it can be written in two ways: with the result below or with the result on top.\n\nFinally, with squares, we saw that multiplying and dividing are two ways of looking at the same arrangement, and that a division can leave a remainder.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
   ],
